      "url": "https://www.invicti.com/learn/directory-traversal-path-traversal",
      "snippet": "UNTRUSTED WEB RESEARCH (not PCAP evidence). Learn what directory (path) traversal is, how dot-dot-slash attacks expose arbitrary files on the server file system, common bypass techniques, ... | Webpage text: Invicti named a Leader in IDC MarketScape: Worldwide DAST 2026 Vendor Assessment. Read now. Cross-site scripting (XSS): Payloads & vulnerability guide Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) Directory listing: vulnerabilities & risks explained Integer overflow: Vulnerabilities, attacks & examples Insecure direct object references (IDOR) OS command injection: Payloads, prevention & attacks Local file inclusion (LFI) SQL injection (SQLi): Prevention, examples & attacks Server-side request forgery (SSRF): Attack types & risks Unvalidated redirects and forwards XML external entity (XXE) injection: Attacks & examples Remote code execution (RCE) Remote file inclusion (RFI) Broken link hijacking (BLH) Cross-frame scripting (XFS) High orbit ion cannon (HOIC) Low Orbit Ion Cannon (LOIC) Man-in-the-middle attacks (MITM) Session hijacking: Detection, defense & prevention Session fixation: Attacks, vulnerabilities & prevention Slowloris DDoS attack: Detection & mitigation Web cache poisoning: Attacks & vulnerabilities explained HTTP strict transport security (HSTS): Examples, misconfigurations & fixes Interactive application security testing (IAST) tools Static application security testing (SAST) Software composition analysis (SCA) Web application firewall (WAF) What is DAST? A guide to dynamic application security testing Directory traversal (path traversal) What is directory traversal? Directory traversal – also called path traversal – is a web vulnerability that lets an attacker access arbitrary files on a server file system outside the web application’s intended directory, such as the document root or an approved upload folder. Directory (path) traversal is possible when an application uses untrusted input to build or select a path on the server file system. Successful attacks can expose sensitive data like configuration files, credentials, logs, or source code, and the information gained is often used to enable follow-on attacks. While traversal is frequently associated with reading files, in some scenarios it can, when combined with other How directory traversal attacks work Directory traversal is a boundary-breaking bug. The application intends to allow access only to a specific directory (for example, a folder of user-uploaded images), but a crafted path causes the application to “escape” that directory and read from elsewhere on the filesystem. The usual starting point for traversal is the web root directory. The importance of the web root Websites and applications run on a web server that serves content from a location called the web document root or web root folder. The document root usually contains subdirectories for each hosted application. On Linux/UNIX with Apache, a typical root folder is /var/www/ On Windows with Microsoft IIS, a typical document root is C:\\inetpub\\wwwroot Developers often write application code that accesses files under the web root or within an application subdirectory. A common example is storing user-uploaded images under a directory such as /var/www/my_app/images/ and then allowing other users to request an image by filename. A typical traversal vulnerability follows a simple pattern: The application accepts a filename or path segment from an HTTP request",
